EXT. NIGHT FERRY - UPPER DECK - NIGHT

Wind drags the black water past the rail in long, unbroken sheets. The ferry's lamps throw a weak yellow circle on the planks, and past that circle there is nothing but weather.

A GIRL, ten, sits on an overturned lifebuoy crate with her knees pulled to her chest. Her coat is two sizes too big and has belonged to someone else. Beside her, upright as a post, stands IGNACIO, seventies, a hat clamped in both fists against the gusts.

GIRL
You said we'd be there by dark.

IGNACIO
It is dark.

GIRL
You know what I mean.

He turns the hat in his hands, a slow revolution, as if he could find the right side of it by feel.

IGNACIO
The captain says the crossing is slower tonight. Currents.

GIRL
You always say the captain.

IGNACIO
Because the captain always says.

A swell lifts the ferry and sets it down hard. The girl's hand shoots out and grips his sleeve. He does not look at it. He lets it stay.

IGNACIO (CONT'D)
What is your name? Your whole name. Tell it to me once more.

GIRL
You know it.

IGNACIO
I want to hear how you say it.

Her chin lifts against the wind.

GIRL
Wren Salazar-Imbert.

IGNACIO
Again. Slower.

WREN
Wren Salazar-Imbert. Wren. Like the bird. Like the small one that makes the loud noise.

IGNACIO
Good. When they ask at the dock, you say it like that.

WREN
Who is going to ask?

He does not answer. The foghorn sounds, once, long enough to shake the rail. Wren tightens her grip on his sleeve, and neither of them looks toward the dark where the shore should be.

WREN (CONT'D)
Ignacio. Will you be there?

IGNACIO
I am here now.

WREN
That is not what I asked.

The ferry rolls. The lamp swings. Ignacio sets his hat on her head, pulling it down over her ears, and she lets him.
